                "**No company here is a clear buy at today's prices. Elecon is the closest, and I move it from own to follow.** India's largest industrial gearbox maker holds 38–40% of its home market (ICRA), about ₹700cr of net cash (company figure; ₹683cr on Screener) and a record order book (₹1,635cr in September 2026, gears up 37%), which includes the largest single industrial-gear order it has ever received. It turned 76% of its operating profit into cash over five years. But its earnings have stalled: FY26 revenue grew 6%, the gear business earned an 18.8% operating margin against the mid-20s three years ago, and management guides only low double-digit revenue growth for FY27 with a flat margin while multinational rivals build Indian plants. Cash conversion slipped to 60% in FY26 as debtor days rose, about ₹70cr of finished goods missed Q1, and its gear exports to the US carry a 50% duty. The price needs about {need:Elecon Engg.Co|19}% a year against my ~15%; when this was written that was a hair on the wrong side of the line. **A fall of a few per cent would make it an own again, provided Q2 converts the order book and the gear margin stops slipping.**",
